cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  SpaceMineralAtlas: crop-specific mineral responses to spaceflight, a
  unit-labelling defect in OSD-655, and a survey of mineral and dietary data
  availability across the NASA Life Sciences Data Archive
message: If you use this software or data, please cite it as below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Barker
    given-names: Richard
    email: dr.richard.barker@gmail.com
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5681-9857'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/dr-richard-barker/SpaceMineralAtlas'
abstract: >-
  Open-data reanalysis of the two NASA Veggie studies with archived ICP-OES
  elemental data (OSD-745 lettuce, OSD-655 Tokyo Bekana) together with
  Inspiration4 clinical chemistry (OSD-575), plus a machine-readable survey of
  all 2,676 NASA Life Sciences Data Archive experiments annotated for mineral
  and dietary content and classified by accessibility. Identifies a 1000-fold
  unit-labelling defect in OSD-655 present in both the data files and the
  archived data dictionary; shows that the two Veggie crops do not share a
  flight ionome response and that apparent calcium and iron increases are
  dry-matter dilution artefacts; and establishes that dietary mineral overload
  from Veggie produce is not achievable at plausible harvest scales.
keywords:
  - spaceflight
  - space nutrition
  - ionome
  - NASA Veggie
  - compositional data analysis
  - OSDR
  - LSDA
  - FAIR data
  - data quality
